@@ -136,7 +136,7 @@ Some features are not supported in AdvCiv-SAS; e.g., non-English languages, pre-
 - **Repo-wide blank-line cleanup (`.py` only):** reduced excessive **consecutive blank lines** to a **single blank line** in Python files with a `(\r?\n)(?:[ \t]*\r?\n){2,}` find to `$1$1` regexp on VS Code. This is a pure formatting no-op (Python ignores blank lines), but it makes large files denser, easier to scan, easier to grep, and more manageable for future review/LLM-assisted cleanup. Comments and code are untouched; only **vertical whitespace** is normalized.
 - A later **horizontal whitespace** pass also **stripped spaces/tabs** from otherwise empty Python lines to reduce invisible diff/lint noise: done with the help of `^[\t ]+$` find regexp with replace all (to nothing) globally on VS Code.
 - Convert **single quote to double quote** (e.g. `'BONUS_IRON'` to `"BONUS_IRON"`), which allowed to **detect and fix incorrect fields** and later strengthen them with `getInfoTypeOrFail(...)`.
-- **Performance & code cleanup:** Broad Python/XML/C++ optimizations (e.g., removed giant ConfigObj docstring (see [KI#21.5](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#215---addressed--fixed---removed-the-giant-configobj-docstring-very-inefficiently-in-the-python-code-and-commented-out-most-if-not-all-as-well)); cached hot values via `static const`/hoisting; normalized inconsistent asset names (see for example [KI#18](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#18---very-inconsistent-naming-in-some-assets))), **map-type heaviness caching** (`CvGame::recomputeMapnameHeaviness`) so land/naval checks run once per game (feeds UNITAI tuning) (see [KI#42](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#42---enhancedaddressed-tune-ais-preferred-based-unitai-based-on-war-status-offense-mode-defense-mode-combined-power-ratios-of-ennemies-etc-if-any-other-general-sanity-checks-and-efficiency-city-size-map-type-land-heavy-vs-water-heavy-vs-other-if-any-for-example-general-suicide-or-such-tendencies-etc), **bonus-specific builds/improvements caching** (e.g. `CvUnitAI::getBonusSpecificLandBuilds`), [AI Personality Panel performance optimization notes](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_AI_Personality_Panel.md#notes-about-performance-optimization-of-the-ai-personality-panel-caching)), **feature-level caching** (AI Personality Panel), refactor **extremely redundant code** (e.g. `refreshUnitSelection` in [CvMilitaryAdvisor.py](/Assets/Python/Screens/CvMilitaryAdvisor.py)), cache redundant computation (e.g. **store per turn and player** top cities' values `CvCityAI::AI_buildingValue`), etc.
+- **Performance & code cleanup:** Broad Python/XML/C++ optimizations (e.g., removed giant ConfigObj docstring (see [KI#21.5](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#215---addressed--fixed---removed-the-giant-configobj-docstring-very-inefficiently-in-the-python-code-and-commented-out-most-if-not-all-as-well)); cached hot values via `static const`/hoisting; normalized inconsistent asset names (see for example [KI#18](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#18---very-inconsistent-naming-in-some-assets))), **map-type heaviness caching** (`CvGame::recomputeMapnameHeaviness`) so land/naval checks run once per game (feeds UNITAI tuning) (see [KI#42](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#42---enhancedaddressed-tune-ais-preferred-based-unitai-based-on-war-status-offense-mode-defense-mode-combined-power-ratios-of-ennemies-etc-if-any-other-general-sanity-checks-and-efficiency-city-size-map-type-land-heavy-vs-water-heavy-vs-other-if-any-for-example-general-suicide-or-such-tendencies-etc), (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5976+) **land/water bonus-specific builds/improvements caching** (`CvPlot::SAS_getBonusSpecificBuild`), [AI Personality Panel performance optimization notes](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_AI_Personality_Panel.md#notes-about-performance-optimization-of-the-ai-personality-panel-caching)), **feature-level caching** (AI Personality Panel), refactor **extremely redundant code** (e.g. `refreshUnitSelection` in [CvMilitaryAdvisor.py](/Assets/Python/Screens/CvMilitaryAdvisor.py)), cache redundant computation (e.g. **store per turn and player** top cities' values `CvCityAI::AI_buildingValue`), etc.
 - Performance optimizations in [Arboria.py](/PrivateMaps/Arboria.py).
 - **Found-value** evaluation now uses `int` end-to-end (CitySiteEvaluator, AIFoundValue, CvPlot cache, SPI, Python callback), not `short`. Why: **avoid overflow/underflow** and short wraparounds in site scoring; keep the logic the same while **improving reliability**. Expectation: more robust site values, fewer edge-case penalties/boosts from short overflow; no known negative gameplay change. Performance: `int` is typically at least as fast as short on modern CPUs; no slowdown expected. Tested: autoplay ran fine. See [Change from short to int the Found value pipeline](/README.md#change-from-short-to-int-the-found-value-pipeline).
 - Removed the old clunky Base AdvCiv's `CONCEPT_TRAIT` XML clutter. Now using the more efficient `WIDGET_PYTHON` similarly instead.
@@ -267,9 +267,8 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5929+) **Unowned bonus extra valuation in city-site valuation:** for example, if we already own Wheat, a site with 1 Maize is **much better for the whole empire** than a city site with 2 more Wheat, which would have **more yields locally for this city** (e.g., ~+3-4 extra food per plot), **but much less long-term empire growth** (e.g., +1 Health +1 Health with Granary for all cities) (which is perhaps better even for this local city). Or, stone, happiness, or other bonuses could provide other advantages, for example **trading, military, accelerating buildables, etc**. Trade imports do not count as owned because they are not permanent. Applied once per bonus type and only after the first city. See [KI#147](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#147---fixedenhanced-base-advciv-issue-of-ai-settlers-not-adding-extra-valuation-to-unowned-bonuses-in-city-site-scoring).
 - **Settler site eval - devalue BFCs with many very bad tiles:** If a candidate city’s BFC contains enough **Peak**, **Ice Cap**, **non-hill Desert (without a bonus)**, or **non-hill Snow (i.e. Ice Sheet) (without a bonus)** tiles, the site is **significantly devalued** (not auto-rejected). See [KI#26.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#262---enhanced-ai-settlers-not-devaluing-very-bad-non-home-tiles-peak-ice-cap-non-hill-desert-without-a-bonus-non-hill-snow-without-a-bonus-if-we-have-enough-of-them-in-bfc).
 - **Respect water bonuses:** if a coast bonus would be in BFC but the site is non-coastal, apply a stronger penalty so we don’t "count" unusable sea resources. Penalty moved from **-165 → -400** (K-Mod used ~-800).
-- **Low-food environments** (tundra/plains/desert/snow/peak counted for safety): even without water bonuses, **prefer coastal sites** so the city can actually grow instead of being stuck at very low pop. See [KI#32](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#32---now-seemingly-fixed-prioritize-settling-on-coast-when-food-environment-is-low-many-tundra-orand-plains-orand-desert-orand-snow-orand-peak-although-i-assume-is-not-counted-but-to-be-safe-the-corinth-screenshot).
+- **Low-food environments**: (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5976+) evaluate food scarcity from each non-home BFC plot's natural food and food added by bonus-specific improvements (e.g., tundra is low food, peak is very low food, but if there are bonuses that give food in the site it reduces the net food-scarcity score (e.g., high tundra high fish/deer may be better than grass/plain hybrid no food from bonuses so do not discard it)). When an inland site has too much net food scarcity, **prefer coastal sites** so the city can grow instead of remaining at very low population. See [KI#32](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#32---now-seemingly-fixed-prioritize-settling-on-coast-when-food-environment-is-low-many-tundra-orand-plains-orand-desert-orand-snow-orand-peak-although-i-assume-is-not-counted-but-to-be-safe-the-corinth-screenshot).
 - **Environment heuristics (non-bonus tiles):** favor sites with lots of grassland/flood plains; penalize plains/desert BFCs. For the tile you settle on, prefer hill-plains/tundra/desert; avoid settling on hill-grass/flood plains unless doing so unlocks a clearly superior multi-bonus BFC. See [KI#26](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#26---seemingly-fixed-for-the-plot-of-the-settled-city-only-in-general-the-ai-should-aim-to-settle-on-a-hill-and-avoid-settling-on-flatland-grassland-or-flood-plains-where-possible-or-during-the-first-city---city-1-when-there-is-no-good-site-yet-to-take).
-- **Settler site eval — devalue BFCs with many *very bad* tiles**: If a candidate city’s BFC contains enough **Peak**, **Ice Cap**, **non-hill Desert (without a bonus)**, or **non-hill Snow (i.e. Ice Sheet) (without a bonus)** tiles, the site is **significantly devalued** (not auto-rejected).
 - **Health tolerance:** don’t reject good jungle sites just for early unhealth. Allow a threshold (more strict for capital, looser later), and consider forest health offsets and flood plains exceptions. Jungle can be chopped later; value the site’s potential, not just immediate health. (Threshold examples: ~≤350 "healthpercent" for capitals; ~≤450 for later cities.)
 - **Spacing:** remove old long-distance penalties that forced tight clusters. Cities are evaluated by distance to the nearest city (not the capital), with penalties only for "too close" or "way too far," strongest early and fading as the empire grows.
 - **Settler evaluation — midgame desert/bonus bias fix**: Disabled the buggy `AIFoundValue::adjustToCivSurroundings` that, from about **turn 51+**, made settlers overvalue founding **on desert bonus tiles** (e.g., **Camel**) or **inside** desert belts. Replaced it with a very small inline pass in its only caller, `AIFoundValue::evaluate`. Observed effect: no longer plants directly on Camel/desert; instead settles **around** large deserts. **Since the spacing change above**, also tightened distance-to-civs and culture-pressure checks to **hard-reject** sites that are **too far** or **heavily pressed** (instead of just penalizing). See [KI#54](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#54---fixed-major-base-advciv---civ4-in-aifoundvalueadjusttocivsurroundings-causing-ai-settlers-to-value-midgame-turn-50-for-example-here-settling-on-camel-desert-worked-around-and-disabled-this-function-entirely-now-inline-a-very-simplified-version-of-it-inline-in-its-only-caller).
@@ -280,7 +279,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city scoring**: value usable food bonuses, fresh-water city tiles, and river support more for the capital than for ordinary later city sites, because early capital growth snowballs into faster tile access, worker/settler production, whipping, and specialists. See [KI#144](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#144---fixed-base-advciv-issue-and-advciv-sas-settler-free-window-follow-up-ai-settlers-sometimes-do-not-move-away-from-a-high-bad-plot-count-start-eg-high-non-bonus-tundra-and-plains-they-now-scout-and-hunt-for-better-not-very-bad-sites-and-no-longer-stop-at-first-good-enough-site-but-instead-now-rerun-evaluate-city-site-again-on-newly-visible-plots-if-a-better-site-eg-more-foodriversfresh-water-exists-nearby-which-we-now-value-more-too-for-first-city-as-well).
 - **Free window:** during the first ~75 turns at Normal speed (scaled by game speed), a "free to produce settler" period lets the capital grow before switching into chosen early strategy (offense, defense, etc.).
 - **First city logic:** settlers consider a larger no-penalty scouting radius for founding the capital (`CvUnitAI::AI_foundFirstCity`), and no longer auto-reject non-coastal/non-river sites if a worse "preferred" site exists. It’ll just pick the **best** overall site. See [KI#43](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#43---attemptingly-improvedenhanced-ai-settlers-for-the-first-city-found-ie-at-turn-0-if-i-am-not-mistaken-settling-too-soon-instead-of-digging-a-bit-for-better-sites-and-other-related-changes).
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city roaming**: Since the first city is especially important, unlike later cities that can be more strategic or semi-decent resource grabs, starting settlers can now **roam briefly for a better first-city site when the current site is bad enough**. This uses a heuristic **BFC bad-plot count** so settlers can move away from clearly bad capital sites without making decent starts wander needlessly, like a human player would: settlers can now spend the bounded early window to **move to visible good-enough sites**, do **safe adjacent scouting for better information**, or **wait if no safe scouting step exists**. Bad plots include no-yield plots, bad water, no-bonus foodless hills (so not hill grassland for example as of now), and no-bonus low-food flat land, while usable/coastal seafood, useful bonus tiles, grass hills, high-yield plots, and workable water are preserved. Empirically, this is notably especially effective in moving away from bad tundra/plain no-bonus heavy starts for the first city.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city roaming**: Since the first city is especially important, unlike later cities that can be more strategic or semi-decent resource grabs, starting settlers can now **roam briefly for a better first-city site when the current site is bad enough**. Settlers can spend the bounded early window to **move to visible good-enough sites**, do **safe adjacent scouting for better information**, or **wait if no safe scouting step exists**. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5976+) Starting-site checks use a shared **potential-food environment score** (using the same logic described under Low-food environments). Food-surplus plots offset poor plots, productive Grass Hills remain neutral, and no-yield peaks/ice receive an extra citizen-unworkable penalty because they are dead BFC slots. This keeps useful resources/coasts from looking poor while still moving away from bad tundra/plains/peak-heavy starts.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city recheck after scouting**: **instead of settling the first good-enough** capital site found **after leaving a very bad start**, the **settler now runs first-city evaluation again** on **nearby visible plots** including **now newly visible** ones first, like a human player would. Empirically, this helped choose even stronger nearby capital sites with more usable food bonuses and/or river support rather than stopping at the first merely-no-longer-bad site. As of now this applies only to the first city, because as of now only the first city is deemed most needing it critically, and later settlers usually know more of the local map so they would generally have a lower need to change route midway (may create other issues too maybe). See [KI#144](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#144---fixed-base-advciv-issue-and-advciv-sas-settler-free-window-follow-up-ai-settlers-sometimes-do-not-move-away-from-a-high-bad-plot-count-start-eg-high-non-bonus-tundra-and-plains-they-now-scout-and-hunt-for-better-not-very-bad-sites-and-no-longer-stop-at-first-good-enough-site-but-instead-now-rerun-evaluate-city-site-again-on-newly-visible-plots-if-a-better-site-eg-more-foodriversfresh-water-exists-nearby-which-we-now-value-more-too-for-first-city-as-well).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5928+) **Naval-heavy-map coastal settling:** On naval-heavy (e.g., Archipelago) maps, settler AI site valuation now optionally add **extra valuation for coastal sites**, as of now especially for the **first city**. This helps early work boat, scouting/contact, trade, first galleys, Moai, or other water-focused buildings; e.g., Cuzco needed a high first-city coastal extra valuation to prefer a coastal Pig + Banana + 2-Sugar river site over a central 4-Sugar river grass site; and kept **moderate for other cities** than the capital after testing showed higher values could, for example, overpull Japan toward tundra coast instead of grass. See [KI#146](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#146---fixedenhanced-base-advciv-issue-of-ai-undervaluing-coastal-settling-on-naval-heavy-maps).
 - **Parking addressed (early game):** Reduced cases of settlers idling in the capital despite safe/urgent expansion, by updating `CvUnitAI::AI_found` to found sooner when conditions allow. In autoplay samples, Spain shifted from **1 city @T100** to **~2 by T60–70** and **~3 by T80–90** (settler production varies somehow, but we move much sooner after we have it now). See [KI#68](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#68---tremendously-improved-ai-having-a-settler-parked-from-turn---45-to-turn-100-and-still-didnt-found-any-city-fixedaddressed-in-cvunitaiai_found).

